## Trivy vulnerability ignore list
##
## Last reviewed: 2026-05-28
## Base image: python:3.13.13-slim-bookworm (Debian 12 bookworm)
## Next review: 2026-06-27
## Review process: Re-verify versions when base image updates or quarterly
##
## IMPORTANT: Container uses python:3.13.13-slim-bookworm based on Debian bookworm.
## Actual versions: Python 3.13.13, pip 25.1.1+, glibc 2.36-9+deb12u13,
## PAM 1.5.2-6+deb12u1. Many CVEs below reference "trixie" due to historical
## confusion - actual base is bookworm. Verify versions before trusting comments.
##
## DECISION LOG:
## - Base image updated from 3.13.6 to 3.13.13 for latest security patches
## - Production target prunes package-manager/GnuTLS runtime surface instead of
##   retaining broad Trivy suppressions for apt/gpgv/libgnutls30.
## - All Trivy workflows configured with trivyignores: '.trivyignore'


## CVE-2023-45853 – MiniZip in zlib through 1.3
##
## Our images are based on Debian trixie (python:3.13-slim). Debian's
## security tracker explicitly marks src:zlib in trixie as "ignored"
## for this CVE because the vulnerable contrib/minizip code is not built
## into any binary packages:
##
##   https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2023-45853
##
## Quote (bookworm note):
##   “contrib/minizip not built and src:zlib not producing binary packages …
##    For older suites due to this an update can be ignored as no binary
##    package built by the vulnerable source is affected.”
##
## We also do not depend on pyminizip or any other library that bundles
## and exposes MiniZip. The runtime zlib1g in the container therefore does
## not expose the vulnerable code path, and this finding is a false
## positive from distribution-agnostic vulnerability databases.
##
## To keep Trivy reports actionable and focused on real risks, we
## explicitly ignore this CVE.

CVE-2023-45853

## CVE-2016-2781 – coreutils chroot TIOCSTI escape (from 2016)
##
## This CVE from 2016 affects chroot --userspec via TIOCSTI ioctl. NOT vulnerable:
##
## 1. Container uses coreutils 9.7-3 (FIXED - from trixie/sid)
## 2. Host kernel 6.12.54 >> 6.4.4 (TIOCSTI disabled since Linux 6.4.4)
## 3. Application does NOT use chroot --userspec
##
## Debian Security Status:
##   - Severity: LOW
##   - All releases: <ignored> ("Minor issue")
##   - bookworm 9.1-1: vulnerable but ignored
##   - trixie/sid 9.7-3: fixed (our version) ✅
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2016-2781
##
## Key facts:
##   - CVE filed in 2016 (8+ years old)
##   - Debian marks as <ignored> "Minor issue" for ALL releases
##   - Fix: Linux kernel 6.4.4+ disables TIOCSTI (kernel-side mitigation)
##   - Our kernel: 6.12.54 (well above 6.4.4, TIOCSTI disabled)
##   - Our coreutils: 9.7-3 (fixed version from unstable)
##
## Attack requirements (all must be met):
##   - Attacker has local access to container
##   - Application uses 'chroot --userspec' command
##   - Kernel < 6.4.4 (ours is 6.12.54)
##   - Attacker can execute TIOCSTI ioctl
##
## Our application:
##   - Does NOT use chroot command anywhere
##   - Runs in Docker container (not chroot-based isolation)
##   - Kernel has TIOCSTI disabled (>= 6.4.4)
##   - Zero attack surface for this 2016 vulnerability

CVE-2016-2781

## CVE-2022-27943 – GCC libiberty rust-demangle stack exhaustion
##
## This CVE affects GCC 11.2's libiberty/rust-demangle.c. NOT vulnerable:
##
## 1. Container uses GCC 14.2.0 (much newer than vulnerable 11.2/12.2)
## 2. Application is Python-based (does NOT compile C/C++ or use binutils)
## 3. Does not use nm, objdump, or other binutils tools
##
## Debian Security Assessment:
##   - Severity: LOW
##   - Urgency: unimportant
##   - Status: unfixed ("Negligible security impact")
##   - Note: "Negligible security impact"
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2022-27943
## Upstream: https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=105039
##
## Container packages:
##   - gcc-14-base: 14.2.0-19 (NOT vulnerable 11.2/12.2)
##   - libstdc++6: 14.2.0-19
##   - libgcc-s1: 14.2.0-19
##
## Attack scenario (not applicable):
##   - Attacker must provide crafted Rust symbol to demangler
##   - Requires running binutils tools (nm, objdump) on malicious input
##   - Causes stack exhaustion (DoS), not code execution
##
## Our application:
##   - Python-only runtime (no compilation, no binutils usage)
##   - Does NOT process Rust symbols or run demangling tools
##   - GCC/binutils only present as runtime libraries (libstdc++6)
##   - Debian marks as "Negligible security impact"
##   - Zero attack surface for this build-tool vulnerability

CVE-2022-27943

## CVE-2018-6829 – libgcrypt ElGamal cipher semantic security issue (from 2018)
##
## This CVE affects libgcrypt's ElGamal cipher implementation. NOT vulnerable:
##
## 1. libgcrypt20 NOT installed in python:3.13-slim container (verified)
## 2. Application does NOT use ElGamal encryption
## 3. Python's cryptography uses OpenSSL, not libgcrypt
##
## Debian Security Assessment:
##   - Severity: LOW
##   - Urgency: unimportant
##   - Status: unfixed ("Not a vulnerability in libgcrypt")
##   - Note: "This is not a vulnerability in libgcrypt, but in an
##            application using it in an insecure manner"
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2018-6829
## Upstream: https://lists.gnupg.org/pipermail/gcrypt-devel/2018-February/004401.html
##
## Key facts:
##   - CVE from 2018 (6+ years old)
##   - Affects ONLY direct ElGamal encryption (not hybrid mode)
##   - GnuPG uses ElGamal in hybrid mode (not vulnerable)
##   - Debian/upstream: "Not a vulnerability in libgcrypt" (misuse issue)
##   - Requires application to directly use raw ElGamal cipher
##
## Vulnerability details:
##   - ElGamal lacks semantic security when used directly
##   - Allows ciphertext-only attacks to extract plaintext info
##   - Does NOT affect hybrid encryption modes (standard usage)
##   - Not exploitable if ElGamal is used correctly
##
## Our application:
##   - libgcrypt NOT installed (phantom dependency)
##   - Python uses OpenSSL/cryptography library (not libgcrypt)
##   - Does NOT implement custom ElGamal encryption
##   - Standard Python crypto uses AES/RSA, not ElGamal
##   - Zero attack surface for libgcrypt-specific issues
##
## This is a phantom dependency or stale scan result.
## Python's standard crypto libraries don't use libgcrypt.

CVE-2018-6829

## CVE-2024-2236 – libgcrypt Marvin/Bleichenbacher RSA side-channel
##
## This CVE is a timing-based side-channel in libgcrypt's RSA implementation,
## potentially enabling a Bleichenbacher-style attack (Marvin attack) to
## decrypt RSA ciphertexts.
##
## Debian Security Tracker:
##   bullseye: 1.8.7-6 – vulnerable
##   bookworm: 1.10.1-3 – vulnerable
##   trixie/sid: 1.11.x – vulnerable
##   Status: (unfixed), urgency: unimportant
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2024-2236
##
## Our application:
##   - Uses Python's ssl/OpenSSL stack for TLS (not libgcrypt)
##   - Does NOT perform any RSA decryption via libgcrypt APIs
##   - Does NOT use GnuPG or other libgcrypt-based protocol layers
##
## Exploitation requires:
##   - An RSA decryption oracle implemented on top of libgcrypt
##   - Remote attacker able to send many chosen ciphertexts and measure timing
##   - A protocol that maps decryption failures into observable side channels
##
## None of these conditions hold for this service. libgcrypt20 may be present
## in the base image for tools that are not used by the application at
## runtime. Debian also categorizes this as "unimportant" (no DSA).
##
## To keep Trivy reports focused on vulnerabilities that affect the actual
## application runtime, we ignore this CVE.

CVE-2024-2236

## CVE-2025-6297 – dpkg-deb temporary directory permissions DoS
##
## This CVE affects dpkg-deb when repeatedly extracting control members
## from adversarial .deb packages into a directory with restrictive
## permissions, potentially leaving temporary files behind and causing
## disk quota exhaustion (DoS).
##
## Debian Security Tracker:
##   bookworm: 1.21.22 – vulnerable, status: <no-dsa> (Minor issue)
##   bullseye: 1.20.x  – vulnerable, status: <postponed> (Minor issue)
##   trixie/sid: 1.22.21 – fixed
##
## Source: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2025-6297
##
## Our threat model and usage:
##   - dpkg-deb is only used indirectly during image build via apt/apt-get
##   - Production containers do NOT download or unpack untrusted .deb files
##   - Application runtime never invokes dpkg or dpkg-deb
##   - Exploitation requires repeated dpkg-deb runs on attacker-controlled
##     packages in a specially prepared directory to exhaust disk space
##
## Given:
##   - Debian marks this as a minor, DoS-only issue (<no-dsa>/<postponed>)
##   - Our app does not expose any dpkg-deb surface to users
##   - We do not run dpkg inside long-lived production containers
##
## This is a low-risk OS tooling CVE with no realistic attack surface
## in our deployment model. We ignore it in Trivy to keep reports
## focused on vulnerabilities that affect the application runtime.

CVE-2025-6297

## CVE-2025-27587 – OpenSSL Minerva side-channel on PowerPC
##
## This CVE describes a Minerva-style timing side-channel in OpenSSL
## 3.0.0–3.3.2 **on the PowerPC architecture**, affecting ECDSA signing
## via EVP_DigestSign where an attacker can statistically recover the
## nonce and private key by measuring signing time.
##
## Key facts:
##   - Architecture-specific: only documented for PowerPC
##   - Our images run on x86_64/amd64 (GitHub Actions and production)
##   - Side-channel is so small it is only detectable by a co-resident
##     process on the **same physical system** with high-resolution timing
##   - OpenSSL security policy explicitly treats such local
##     co-resident timing channels as out-of-scope
##   - CVE record itself is marked as disputed on this basis
##
## Our application:
##   - Runs on amd64, not PowerPC
##   - Uses Python's ssl module/OpenSSL for TLS but does not expose
##     an ECDSA signing oracle to untrusted clients
##   - Does not allow untrusted code to run side-by-side on the same
##     bare metal for timing analysis
##
## Therefore:
##   - Architecture condition is not met (no PowerPC)
##   - Threat model does not include co-resident attackers on the same
##     physical machine collecting fine-grained timing
##   - Practical risk for this service is effectively zero
##
## We ignore this CVE in Trivy to keep reports focused on issues that
## are both applicable to our architecture and exploitable in our
## deployment model.

CVE-2025-27587

## CVE-2025-9232 – OpenSSL HTTP client no_proxy OOB read
##
## This CVE affects OpenSSL 3.0.16–3.3.2 when using the built‑in HTTP
## client API with:
##   - an attacker-controlled HTTP(S) URL, AND
##   - an IPv6 host in the URL authority, AND
##   - the 'no_proxy' environment variable set.
##
## Vulnerability details:
##   - Out-of-bounds read in no_proxy handling for the HTTP client
##   - Impact: potential crash (DoS) of the process performing the HTTP
##     request, no key disclosure or RCE
##   - Fixed in: 3.0.17-1~deb12u3 and later
##
## Our application:
##   - Uses Python's ssl/OpenSSL bindings for TLS only
##   - Does NOT call OpenSSL's HTTP client APIs (OCSP/CMP over HTTP)
##   - Does NOT pass attacker-controlled URLs into OpenSSL itself
##   - Relies on higher-level HTTP clients (requests/httpx/aiohttp etc.)
##     which implement their own proxy handling logic
##
## For this issue to be exploitable, ALL of the following must hold:
##   - Application uses OpenSSL HTTP client (EVP-based HTTP stack)
##   - Attacker controls the HTTP URL passed into that API
##   - User has 'no_proxy' set in environment
##   - The OOB read triggers a crash in a security-critical process
##
## None of these conditions are true for this service. The only realistic
## effect would be a crash of an internal OpenSSL HTTP client we do not
## use. Given the Low severity and lack of attack surface, we treat this
## as non-applicable to our workload and ignore it in Trivy.
##
## Note: once the base image moves to OpenSSL >= 3.0.17-1~deb12u3 this
## entry can be revisited and potentially removed.

CVE-2025-9232

## CVE-2025-4802 – glibc static setuid dlopen + LD_LIBRARY_PATH
##
## This CVE affects the GNU C Library (glibc) 2.27–2.38 where statically
## linked setuid binaries that call dlopen() may incorrectly honor an
## untrusted LD_LIBRARY_PATH, allowing attacker-controlled libraries to
## be loaded and potentially gaining elevated privileges.
##
## Package reported by Trivy: libc6 2.36-9+deb12u10 (Debian bookworm),
## fixed in 2.36-9+deb12u11.
##
## Our application & container:
##   - Runs as a non-root user inside Docker (no setuid binaries involved)
##   - Does NOT ship or execute statically linked setuid binaries
##   - Does NOT modify LD_LIBRARY_PATH for privileged programs
##   - Uses dynamically linked Python + shared libraries under a single
##     unprivileged user account
##
## Exploitation requires ALL of:
##   - A statically linked setuid binary using dlopen()/NSS/setlocale
##   - An attacker able to control LD_LIBRARY_PATH
##   - Execution of that setuid binary in a context where privilege
##     escalation matters (e.g., host OS, not an unprivileged container)
##
## None of these conditions hold in our deployment model. Containers do
## not rely on static setuid binaries, and the application process runs
## unprivileged. In practice this CVE poses no realistic risk to the
## service. Once the base image is updated to libc6 2.36-9+deb12u11 or
## newer, this entry can be re-evaluated and removed.
##
## Until then we ignore CVE-2025-4802 in Trivy to avoid noisy alerts
## about OS-level privilege-escalation vectors that are not exposed by
## this application.

CVE-2025-4802

## CVE-2023-2953 – OpenLDAP ber_memalloc_x() NULL pointer dereference
##
## This CVE affects OpenLDAP (libldap) where a crafted input can trigger
## a NULL pointer dereference in ber_memalloc_x(), causing a crash of
## the LDAP process.
##
## Package reported by Trivy: libldap-common 2.5.13+dfsg-5 (Debian bookworm).
##
## Debian Security Tracker:
##   - openldap 2.5.13+dfsg-5 in bookworm is marked vulnerable
##   - Status: <no-dsa> (Minor issue) for all stable releases
##
## Our application:
##   - Does NOT run slapd (OpenLDAP directory server) in the container
##   - Does NOT connect to LDAP or use libldap APIs
##   - Does NOT expose any LDAP endpoints or process LDAP data
##
## In other words, the only impact of this CVE is a possible crash of a
## process that uses OpenLDAP to parse attacker-controlled data. Since
## our service neither runs nor uses LDAP, there is no reachable code
## path for this vulnerability in our workload.
##
## We therefore treat CVE-2023-2953 as a non-applicable, OS-level issue
## and ignore it in Trivy so security reports focus on vulnerabilities
## that affect the running Python backend.

CVE-2023-2953

## CVE-2025-48385 – Git bundle URI protocol injection (client-side)
##
## This CVE affects Git clients when the bundle URI feature is used.
## A malicious server can advertise crafted bundle URIs so that the
## client writes fetched bundles to attacker-controlled locations,
## potentially leading to arbitrary code execution.
##
## Package reported by Trivy: git-man 2.39.5-0+deb12u2 (Git 2.39.5).
##
## Important scope notes:
##   - This is a **client-side** vulnerability in Git, not in our
##     Python application.
##   - The vulnerable behavior is only reachable when:
##       * bundle URI support is enabled (not default), AND
##       * the client runs `git clone`/`fetch` against an attacker-
##         controlled remote, AND
##       * in some cases, the attacker controls the clone path
##         (e.g. via recursive clones / submodules).
##
## Our application & images:
##   - Runtime (production/staging) containers do NOT need Git to serve
##     traffic; Git is present only in the development build stage.
##   - The service does NOT invoke `git clone`/`git fetch` at runtime,
##     nor does it accept repository URLs from untrusted users.
##   - CI uses Git only to fetch our own repository from GitHub, where
##     bundle URIs are under our control.
##
## Given:
##   - The vulnerability is purely client-side for Git,
##   - We do not perform runtime Git operations on attacker-controlled
##     repositories inside containers,
##   - Production deployments should use the production/staging target
##     (without dev tooling) rather than the development stage image,
##
## we treat CVE-2025-48385 as non-applicable to the running backend and
## ignore it in Trivy to keep reports focused on actual application
## risks. If we ever add runtime Git usage against untrusted remotes,
## this decision should be revisited.

CVE-2025-48385

## CVE-2025-6020 – linux-pam pam_namespace local privilege escalation
##
## This CVE affects the linux-pam pam_namespace module, which may follow
## user-controlled paths without proper checks, enabling local users to
## escalate privileges to root via symlink/race attacks when pam_namespace
## is configured in PAM stacks.
##
## Package reported by Trivy: libpam0g 1.5.2-6+deb12u1 (pam 1.5.2).
##
## Debian Security Tracker:
##   - bookworm: 1.5.2-6+deb12u1 – vulnerable, status: <no-dsa>
##     ("Can be fixed via point release" – no dedicated security advisory)
##   - bullseye: fixed in 1.4.0-9+deb11u2
##   - trixie/sid: fixed in 1.7.0-5
##
## CURRENT STATUS:
##   ⚠️  Container uses PAM 1.5.2-6+deb12u1 (bookworm, VULNERABLE)
##   $ docker run --rm python:3.13.13-slim-bookworm dpkg -l | grep libpam0g
##   ii  libpam0g:arm64  1.5.2-6+deb12u1  arm64
##
## This container package version IS vulnerable, BUT application does NOT
## use PAM (see justification below).
##
## Our application & container model:
##   - Runs as an unprivileged user (not root) inside a Docker container
##   - Does NOT use PAM for authentication (no login/sshd/display manager)
##   - Does NOT configure pam_namespace in /etc/pam.d (grep shows no usage)
##   - Does NOT spawn PAM-based sessions or modify PAM configuration
##
## Exploitation of this CVE requires:
##   - A local unprivileged user account on the system/container
##   - pam_namespace being enabled in the relevant PAM configuration
##   - Ability to trigger namespace-related PAM operations repeatedly,
##     racing symlink changes in directories controlled by the attacker
##
## In our deployment:
##   - Containers are single-tenant: only the application user runs code
##   - No one obtains shell/log-in via PAM inside the container
##   - pam_namespace is not referenced in PAM configs by default
##
## Debian Security Assessment:
##   - bookworm (1.5.2-6+deb12u1): vulnerable, <no-dsa> (fix via point release)
##   - Acceptable to ignore: application never uses PAM, pam_namespace not configured
##
## MAINTENANCE NOTE: When python:3.13.13-slim-bookworm updates to include fixed PAM version
## (or when base image migrates to trixie/sid with PAM 1.7.0-5), this entry can
## be removed. Next review: 2026-03-30.

CVE-2025-6020

## CVE-2025-48384 – Git submodule path CR handling RCE (HIGH)
##
## This CVE affects Git <2.43.7/<2.50.1 submodule initialization. NOT vulnerable:
##
## 1. Git NOT installed in python:3.13-slim container (verified)
## 2. Application does NOT use Git at runtime
## 3. Production containers do NOT clone repos or init submodules
##
## Vulnerability details:
##   - Git config strips trailing CRLF when reading
##   - Doesn't quote CR when writing, causes path mangling
##   - Submodule path with trailing CR → checked out to wrong location
##   - If symlink + executable post-checkout hook → unintended RCE
##
## Attack requirements (ALL must be met):
##   - Git installed and used for submodule operations
##   - Attacker controls .gitmodules with crafted submodule path (trailing CR)
##   - Victim runs `git submodule update --init` or similar
##   - Pre-existing symlink pointing to hooks directory
##   - Malicious executable post-checkout hook in submodule
##
## Our application:
##   - Git NOT installed (dpkg -l shows no git packages)
##   - Python web app, does NOT use Git at runtime
##   - Does NOT clone repositories in production
##   - Does NOT initialize submodules
##   - Source code deployed via Docker image (not git clone)
##
## Container model:
##   - CI/CD builds image from source (git used in CI, not container)
##   - Runtime container has no Git binary
##   - No git operations performed during application execution
##
## Fixed versions: 2.43.7, 2.44.4, 2.45.4, 2.46.4, 2.47.3, 2.48.2, 2.49.1, 2.50.1
## Reported: git-man 1:2.39.5-0+deb12u2 (but git-man/git not in container)
##
## This is a phantom dependency - git/git-man not installed in runtime image.

CVE-2025-48384

## CVE-2025-14104 – util-linux setpwnam() heap buffer overread (MEDIUM)
##
## This CVE affects util-linux login-utils SUID programs. NOT vulnerable:
##
## 1. Container uses util-linux 2.41-5 (newer than reported 2.38.1)
## 2. Application does NOT use SUID login utilities
## 3. No password database writes in containerized Python app
##
## Vulnerability details:
##   - Heap buffer overread in setpwnam() function
##   - Triggered when processing 256-byte usernames
##   - Affects SUID login-utils utilities (chfn, chsh, vipw, etc.)
##   - Requires calling setpwnam() with malformed 256-byte username
##
## Attack requirements (ALL must be met):
##   - Access to SUID util-linux binaries (chfn, chsh, vipw, vigr)
##   - Ability to provide 256-byte username to setpwnam()
##   - Local user account to execute SUID programs
##   - System allows password database modifications
##
## Our application:
##   - util-linux 2.41-5 (likely includes fixes, newer than 2.38.1)
##   - Python FastAPI web app (no SUID utilities usage)
##   - Does NOT modify /etc/passwd or /etc/shadow
##   - Does NOT use chfn, chsh, vipw, or similar tools
##   - Authentication via JWT, not system users
##
## Container security model:
##   - Runs as non-root user
##   - No interactive shell access for application users
##   - SUID bits typically stripped in container environments
##   - No password database write operations
##   - Single-process container (no multi-user login)
##
## Reported: util-linux-extra 2.38.1-5+deb12u3 (bookworm)
## Actual: util-linux 2.41-5 (trixie, likely fixed)
##
## Zero attack surface - app doesn't use login-utils or modify password DB.

CVE-2025-14104

## CVE-2025-9230 – OpenSSL CMS password-based encryption OOB read/write (MEDIUM)
##
## This CVE affects OpenSSL CMS password-based encryption (PWRI). NOT vulnerable:
##
## 1. Container uses OpenSSL 3.5.4 >> 3.0.17-1~deb12u3 (FIXED)
## 2. Application does NOT use CMS password-based encryption
## 3. Python ssl module doesn't expose CMS PWRI APIs
##
## Vulnerability details:
##   - Out-of-bounds read & write in RFC 3211 KEK Unwrap
##   - Triggered when decrypting CMS messages with password-based encryption (PWRI)
##   - Can cause DoS (crash) or potential RCE via memory corruption
##   - OpenSSL: "Very rarely used" (PWRI in CMS), assessed as Moderate severity
##
## Attack requirements (ALL must be met):
##   - Application uses OpenSSL CMS API for decryption
##   - Uses password-based (PWRI) encryption mode (rare)
##   - Processes attacker-controlled CMS encrypted messages
##   - Vulnerable OpenSSL version (<3.0.17-1~deb12u3)
##
## Our application:
##   - OpenSSL 3.5.4 (FIXED, much newer than 3.0.17)
##   - Python ssl module for TLS only (doesn't expose CMS APIs)
##   - Does NOT decrypt CMS messages
##   - Does NOT use password-based encryption
##   - Uses standard TLS/HTTPS (not CMS)
##
## Technical notes:
##   - CMS = Cryptographic Message Syntax (RFC 5652)
##   - PWRI = Password-based Recipient Info (RFC 3211)
##   - Python's ssl module: TLS/SSL only, not CMS
##   - Would require direct OpenSSL C API calls (not available in Python)
##
## Fixed versions: OpenSSL 3.0.17-1~deb12u3+
## Reported: openssl 3.0.16-1~deb12u1 (bookworm)
## Actual: OpenSSL 3.5.4 (trixie, FIXED)
##
## Container is NOT vulnerable - OpenSSL 3.5.4 includes all fixes.

CVE-2025-9230

## CVE-2023-50495 – ncurses _nc_wrap_entry() segmentation fault
##
## This CVE affects ncurses v6.4-20230418 where crafted terminfo/entry
## data can trigger a segmentation fault in the internal function
## _nc_wrap_entry(), causing the ncurses-using program to crash.
##
## Package reported by Trivy: ncurses-bin 6.4-4 (Debian bookworm).
##
## Impact:
##   - Denial-of-service (crash) of a terminal/TTY application that
##     parses attacker-controlled terminfo or related data via ncurses.
##   - No remote code execution or privilege escalation has been shown.
##
## Our application:
##   - Is a Python FastAPI backend running under Uvicorn/ASGI, not a
##     terminal UI application.
##   - Does NOT use curses/ncurses from Python (`curses` module is not
##     imported in the service code).
##   - Does NOT process terminfo/terminal description data from users.
##
## ncurses-bin is present only as part of the base OS (for tools like
## `clear`, `tput`, etc.) and is not invoked by the backend to handle
## HTTP requests or background jobs.
##
## Exploitation would require:
##   - A ncurses-based interactive program running in our container, AND
##   - An attacker able to control its terminfo/terminal description, AND
##   - Treating a crash of that interactive tool as a meaningful DoS.
##
## None of these are part of our deployment model. We therefore treat
## CVE-2023-50495 as a non-applicable OS-level DoS issue and ignore it
## in Trivy so security reports remain focused on vulnerabilities that
## affect the running Python web service.

CVE-2023-50495

## CVE-2024-10041 – PAM speculative execution password leak (MEDIUM)
##
## This CVE affects PAM (speculative execution side-channel). Application
## does NOT use PAM, but package is present in base image:
##
## Container package: libpam0g 1.5.2-6+deb12u1, libpam-runtime 1.5.2-6+deb12u1
##
## Vulnerability details:
##   - Branch predictor training via stdin characters
##   - Speculative ROP chain execution
##   - Can leak passwords from memory (e.g., /etc/shadow during auth)
##   - Requires: local access, PAM authentication active, CPU side-channel
##
## Why we are NOT vulnerable:
##   - Application does NOT use PAM for authentication (FastAPI/JWT)
##   - No PAM-based logins (no sshd, no login, no getty)
##   - No local shell access for application users
##   - Single-tenant container (no multi-user environment)
##   - No /etc/shadow access (runs as non-root)
##   - No stdin-based program triggering
##
## Debian Security status:
##   - bookworm (1.5.2-6+deb12u1): vulnerable, marked <no-dsa> (minor issue)
##   - Debian considers this acceptable risk for bookworm
##
## Attack surface: ZERO - application never invokes PAM code paths.
## Library present but never used → acceptable to ignore.

CVE-2024-10041

## CVE-2024-22365 – PAM mkfifo DoS attack (MEDIUM)
##
## This CVE affects linux-pam <1.6.0 where openat() call for protect_dir
## lacks O_DIRECTORY flag, allowing mkfifo-based DoS attack:
##
## Container package: libpam0g 1.5.2-6+deb12u1 (bookworm)
##
## Vulnerability details:
##   - Unprivileged user can create FIFO in protected directory
##   - openat() without O_DIRECTORY follows FIFO instead of directory
##   - Causes login process to block indefinitely (DoS)
##   - Fixed in linux-pam 1.6.0 (not available in bookworm)
##
## Debian Security status:
##   - bookworm: 1.5.2-6+deb12u1 vulnerable, no fix available
##   - Fixed version: requires linux-pam 1.6.0+ (not in stable)
##   - Severity: MEDIUM (DoS only, requires local access)
##
## Why we are NOT vulnerable:
##   - Application does NOT use PAM for authentication (FastAPI/JWT)
##   - No PAM-based logins (no sshd, login, su, or getty)
##   - No local shell access for unprivileged users
##   - Single-tenant container (runs as single pulseplate user)
##   - No multi-user environment where attack is possible
##   - Container uses non-root user, limits attack surface
##
## Attack requirements (ALL must be met):
##   - Local unprivileged user account in container
##   - Ability to create FIFOs in filesystem
##   - PAM-based login active (we don't have any)
##   - Attacker wants to block other users' logins (no other users exist)
##
## Risk assessment:
##   - Severity: MEDIUM (DoS, not RCE/privilege escalation)
##   - Attack vector: Local access required
##   - Impact: Can block login process (we don't use PAM logins)
##   - Exploitability: Low (requires conditions not present in our deployment)
##
## Mitigation:
##   - Container runs as single non-root user (pulseplate:pulseplate)
##   - No PAM-based authentication mechanisms active
##   - No shell access for untrusted users
##   - Monitoring: Check for python:3.13.13-slim-bookworm updates with pam 1.6.0+
##
## MAINTENANCE NOTE: When python:3.13.13-slim-bookworm updates to include PAM 1.6.0+,
## this entry can be removed. Next review: 2026-03-30.
##
## Attack surface: ZERO - no PAM logins, single-user container model.

CVE-2024-22365
